							"amount": 1
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "iq",
							"amount": -2
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "ht",
							"amount": 3
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "hp",
							"amount": 4
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tAteWpU0T5f-Ue3EY",
					"name": "Increased Basic Move (Ork)",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Ork legs are short, but they cover ground at a lumbering, relentless gallop: a mob at full charge outruns a marching human line and is on top of it before it can reload. Codex background and Ork fiction alike treat the onrushing Waaagh! as a stampede, not a march.\n\nDesign: Increased Basic Move +3 (B17, 5/level, 15 points): Basic Speed 6.0 gives a Boy Move 6, so +3 brings the Ork baseline to Move 9, faster than a human's 6-7 without approaching an Eldar's.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 15,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "basic_move",
							"amount": 3
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "taTsUzbiTkLD4icgE",
					"name": "Thick Hide",
					"reference": "B46",
					"local_notes": "DR 2, all locations. Stacks with worn armour.\n\nLore: An Ork's skin is thick, rubbery and mottled green, tough enough on its own to turn a knife-edge or a glancing shot that would open a human. Every depiction of an unarmoured Ork brawling, in Codex background and Ork fiction alike, treats a bit of greenskin hide as meaningfully tougher than bare human flesh, well before any scrap armour goes on top of it.\n\nDesign: Damage Resistance 2 (B46, 5/level, all locations, 10 points): modest next to the Ossmodula's partial DR 3 or Custodes-tier toughness, but applies everywhere and is on top of whatever armour an Ork straps on.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 10,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "dr_bonus",
							"locations": [
								"all"
							],
							"amount": 2
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tcNDSvxL-yq4HhCds",
					"name": "High Pain Threshold",
					"reference": "B59",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Orks shrug off wounds, keep fighting with an arm hanging by a tendon, and treat a lost eye as a minor inconvenience worth a good story later — a running joke across every Ork-focussed novel and a staple of Codex background (\"'e's still comin'!\").\n\nDesign: Basic Set High Pain Threshold (B59), 10 points, unmodified.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "t6-ubLeVPW-CvxM2m",
					"name": "Hard to Kill",
					"reference": "B58",
					"local_notes": "+3 to HT rolls made solely to avoid death.\n\nLore: An Ork that should be dead — gutshot, missing limbs, skull dented in — routinely keeps swinging until the fight is over, and often survives afterwards besides. Ghazghkull Thraka: Prophet of the Waaagh! plays this for both horror and comedy in the same scene more than once.\n\nDesign: Hard to Kill 3 (B58, 2/level, 6 points): +3 to death checks, below a Warboss's own extra levels below.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 2,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 3,
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "tlRrawanJxbrNhSzT",
					"name": "Rapid Healing",
					"reference": "B79",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Wounds that would fester or kill close over in days rather than weeks — part of the same fungal biology that lets a beaten Ork mob be back at strength within a season. Codex background is explicit that Ork tissue heals unnaturally fast; Black Library plays it as \"give it a week and 'e'll be grand.\"\n\nDesign: Basic Set Rapid Healing (B79, 5 points): doubles effective HT for natural recovery rolls.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 5
				},
				{
					"id": "t3yRu0He6oO1pooY9",
					"name": "Thick Skull",
					"reference": "B46",
					"local_notes": "DR 4 on the skull only, on top of Thick Hide (skull DR 6 in total). Orks have brains and take normal extra injury from skull and eye hits once something gets through. House rule, not a priced trait: an Ork who loses a limb, or per the fiction even the head, to a crippling wound is not necessarily dead from that alone. A Painboy who recovers the part and staples or wires it back on within a scene or two restores it to function once the patient heals, using Surgery.\n\nLore: Ork skulls are notoriously thick, and Orks survive head wounds that would kill anything else, up to having a head sewn back on by a Dok (Codex background and a long tradition of \"'e's had worse\" anecdotes). They do have brains: a bolt through the eye still kills an Ork.\n\nDesign: Damage Resistance 4 (B46, 5/level, 20) with Partial (skull only): the GCS Basic Set data prices Partial at -10% per -1 of the location's hit penalty, and the skull is -7, so -70%: 20 x 0.3 = 6 points. Replaced an earlier Injury Tolerance (No Brain), at the user's direction, because it made headshots meaningless against a species that does have a brain.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "m1pdHrU1SAavHxZ1P",
							"name": "Partial (Skull only)",
							"reference": "B47",
							"cost_adj": "-70%"
						}
					],
					"base_points": 20,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "dr_bonus",
							"locations": [
								"skull"
							],
							"amount": 4
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tYt21gu9h2T9tmRuN",
					"name": "Resistant to Disease and Toxins",
					"reference": "B81",
					"local_notes": "Resistant (Common: Disease and Toxins), Immunity.\n\nLore: An Ork can eat rotten meat, drink promethium-cut moonshine and wade through toxic industrial runoff without noticeable harm — Codex background treats Ork constitutions as effectively immune to the diseases and poisons that trouble other races, part of the same fungal-symbiote biology that lets a Squig be dinner, transport or weapon depending on how it's grown.\n\nDesign: Resistant (B81), Common: Disease and Toxins, Immunity tier — base 15, x1 for Immunity = 15 points, the same tier and math this project's Oolitic Kidney entry uses for a single unified hazard category, pitched below the Custodes' broader Very Common tier (30) since Orks are shown getting stinking drunk and mildly green-sick on purpose for fun, not as flatly unaffected as a Custodian.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 15
				},
				{
					"id": "tZ4QKCsBe0OgwXR5V",
					"name": "Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction)",
					"reference": "P53",
					"local_notes": "Divisor 2 (level = divisor): after DR and wounding multipliers, all injury is divided by 2 (round down, minimum 1 if anything penetrated). The Nob and Warboss growth lenses raise the divisor through \"Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction) upgrade\" levels; add the upgrade levels to this trait's 2 for the total divisor.\n\nLore: Orks survive and fight on through wounds that would kill a man: Codex: Orks background is full of Boyz who keep charging with holes in them, and the fungal-hardy greenskin body simply does not register damage the way a human one does. A big Ork is tougher still (see the growth lenses).\n\nDesign: Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction) (GURPS Powers p. 53, 25 points per level, level = divisor): divisor 2 = 50 points. Bought as levels rather than a flat cost so the growth lenses can raise the divisor one step at a time. It is what keeps a Boy standing after two lasgun hits and makes bolt-pistol rounds a real but not instantly fatal threat, on top of Thick Hide's DR and the extra HP.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 25,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 2,
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "txYrAvTpDnsz1PgLX",
					"name": "Fungal Physiology (Spore-Born)",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Orks are not born but grown: an Ork reproduces by releasing spores that seed fresh fungal growth wherever conditions allow (a mechanism shared with Gretchin, Squigs and the rest of the Ork ecosystem), has no biological sex, and needs neither a mate nor, ordinarily, a trained physician who isn't another Ork with a saw and a good attitude. This is consistent, well-established Codex background and is played straight rather than for shock across Black Library's Ork fiction — an Ork simply is an ambulatory fungal colony shaped like a green thug.\n\nDesign: 0-point lore flag. Every mechanical payoff of this biology (thick hide, extreme pain tolerance, fast healing, survivability of wounds that would kill outright, immunity to disease and poison) is priced separately above; this entry exists purely to document why an Ork needs no gender, no mate, and no bedside manner from anyone.",
					"tags": [
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "t_zhFTfihUNwA3G9g",
					"name": "Bloodlust",
					"reference": "B125",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Fighting is not a means to an end for an Ork, it is the point of being alive: Orks pick fights they don't need to, press an attack past the point of sense, and are consistently written (Ghazghkull Thraka above all) as unable to simply walk away from a fight worth having.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Bloodlust (B125), self-control 12, -10 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": -10,
					"cr": 12
				},
				{
					"id": "teKdXADwn_67wBWwx",
					"name": "Impulsiveness",
					"reference": "B139",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Orks act first and, on a good day, think about it afterwards — a running theme in how Ork plans in the fiction go from \"good idea\" to \"already happening\" with nothing in between.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Impulsiveness (B139), self-control 12, -10 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": -10,
					"cr": 12
				},
				{
					"id": "tFZne3APPdkw00wy1",
					"name": "Overconfidence",
					"reference": "B148",
					"local_notes": "Lore: An Ork mob genuinely believes it can win any fight it's in, right up until (and sometimes past) the point it's losing badly — a big part of why Ork strategy in the fiction is so often \"charge and find out.\"\n\nDesign: Basic Set Overconfidence (B148), self-control 12, -5 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": -5,
					"cr": 12
				},
				{
					"id": "toj-C9_0lW_aG2Q0f",
					"name": "Ork Kunnin'",
					"reference": "B89",
					"local_notes": "This project's Talent (B89) for practical battlefield cunning: adds\nits level to Tactics, Traps, Camouflage and Observation, the same way\nthe Psyker Talent (Imperium/Traits/Psykers and the Warp.adq) adds to\nits own listed skills, per that file's house extension of Talent's\nnormal rules. An Ork Boy is not clever in the abstract (IQ 8 is IQ\n8), but a mob that has survived a hundred scraps has a bone-deep,\npractical instinct for where the enemy is weak, where the cover is,\nand when a trap will work — instinct this Talent represents directly\nrather than through raw IQ.\n\nLore: Ork tactics look like chaos from outside and are, by any human military-doctrine standard, chaos — and yet Ork mobs consistently find the gap in a defence, spring an ambush, or route around a strongpoint with an efficiency that surprises Imperial commanders who underestimate them. Codex background and Ork fiction play this as real, if inarticulate, battlefield cunning rather than dumb luck.\n\nDesign: Talent affecting four skills sits at the Medium Talent rate (B90, 10 points/level, 3-5 skill bracket), the same rate this project's Psyker Talent uses for the same reason. Base entry costs 0 points at level 0; every template above Gretchin buys at least level 1 (10 points, included in the Ork Physiology package below).",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Talent",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 10,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 1,
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "tooRJFb016NbfNPi3",
					"name": "WAAAGH! Gestalt",
					"local_notes": "Roughly ten or more fellow Orks fighting nearby, in earshot of the\nsame battle-roar, count as a WAAAGH!. While inside one:\n- Fright Checks and Perils of the Warp rolls get +2 (the conditional\n  bonus above).\n- Ork-built weapons and vehicles the character carries or crews have\n  their Malf number raised by 1 (fewer jams; e.g. Malf 14 becomes 15,\n  the Zzap Gun's 12 becomes 13). A lone Ork, or one with fewer than\n  ten fellows in earshot, gets no bonus. This is the mechanical hook\n  for \"Orks believe it, so it works, and it works better the more of\n  them believe it together.\"\n- A Weirdboy treats his own Warp Empowerment as one level higher (cap\n  +10) for the purpose of which known powers he may safely cast.\n  The Weirdboy template now buys Empowerment 20 (cap 210) for the\n  'Eadbanger, which already covers Da Jump (60) at any time, so the\n  bonus only matters to a Weirdboy who has dropped his Empowerment\n  below 5 (see the Weirdboy template).\n\nLore: A lone Ork is dangerous; a mob of Orks worked up into a full WAAAGH! is a weaponised belief field — braver, louder, and (per Codex background's long-running joke about Ork technology) more likely to have its crude guns and rickety vehicles actually function, because enough Orks are collectively certain that they will. Weirdboyz in particular are consistently shown drawing directly on this collective psychic roar to power their biggest, most dangerous tricks; a Weirdboy isolated from a WAAAGH! is a much smaller threat.\n\nDesign: A conditional Fright Check/Perils bonus eyeballed against Basic's Fearlessness (2 points per level, so a flat +2 is 4 points, B55), restricted to a common-in-play situation (a mob of ten or more), which alone would price at about 2-3 points. The 5 points also buy two mechanical riders that are the point of the trait: an Ork-tech Malf +1 inside a WAAAGH!, and the Weirdboy's Empowerment +1. The Empowerment rider is not separately priced beyond that since it only changes which already-bought powers a Weirdboy may safely reach for.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 5,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "conditional_modifier",
							"situation": "to resist Fright Checks and Perils of the Warp, while fighting as part of a large mob of fellow Orks (a WAAAGH!)",
							"amount": 2
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tXwiShQ-Lc79xddxd",
					"name": "Fearlessness",
					"reference": "B55",
					"local_notes": "Lore: An Ork has no fear to speak of: a lone Boy walks at a line of Marines because it looks like a good fight. It is the Gretchin who run. Below the Warboss's own extra levels, but enough that a Boy with Will 8 no longer fails a Fright Check as readily as a Guardsman.\n\nDesign: Fearlessness 3 (B55, 2/level, 6 points), a flat +3 to Fright Checks and Will rolls to resist fear and intimidation, on top of WAAAGH! Gestalt's conditional bonus.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 2,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 3,
					"base_points": 0
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "tMEAiZbhHaGk0AcsF",
			"name": "Unusual Background (Latent Psychic Potential)",
			"reference": "B96",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Psychic ability is a random, rare mutation of the human genome that lets its bearer perceive and touch the immaterium (the warp). Sources disagree on exact frequency — Imperial administrative texts and Dark Heresy-line background both describe it as \"rare but not vanishingly so,\" on the order of a handful of latent potentials per hive-sized population, with usably strong psykers far rarer still. Treat any precise ratio as flavour, not census data.\n\nDesign: Required to take Psyker (Talent), Warp Empowerment, or any trait carrying the Warp Sorcery power modifier below. Unusual Background (B96) leaves its cost to the GM; 10 points reflects that the ability itself is not learned but grants access to an entire framework of further abilities, roughly the going rate other published settings use for \"you may buy from this power list at all.\"",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": 10
		},
		{
			"id": "toB5TTbrXkFuZgj4j",
			"name": "Psyker",
			"reference": "TSOR5",
			"local_notes": "This project's Sorcery Talent (Thaumatology: Sorcery, TSOR5) analogue:\napplies as Sorcery Talent applies to a sorcerer's skills in TSOR, just\nto a 40k-specific list — Psyniscience, Meditation, Hidden Lore (the\nWarp), and the Will roll to resist Perils of the Warp (see above) — a\nhouse extension of Talent's normal \"add to listed skills\" rule,\nprecedented by Magery's and Sorcery Talent's own habit of aiding\nrelated rolls beyond pure spellcasting. Reaction bonus: +1/level from\nother sanctioned psykers and the Astra Telepathica.\n\nPsyker (Talent) and Warp Empowerment are separate purchases on separate\naxes, exactly as Sorcery Talent and Sorcerous Empowerment are in TSOR:\nthis trait only sharpens related skills and Perils resistance, while\nWarp Empowerment (above) is what actually gates which powers a psyker\nmay know. A high-Talent, low-Empowerment psyker is a sharp, well-drilled\nmind who simply hasn't learned much yet; the reverse is a raw prodigy\nwho is clumsy with the skills around their gift.\n\nImperial Assignment scale (Adeptus Astra Telepathica / Black Ships),\nweakest to strongest, mapped to Talent level. See Warp Empowerment above\nfor the matching Empowerment level/point-cap column, which is the real\ngate on ability selection:\n  Iota    — Talent 0 (latent; register but rarely take Psyker traits at all)\n  Zeta    — Talent 1\n  Eta     — Talent 1\n  Theta   — Talent 2\n  Epsilon — Talent 2  (typical Wyrdvane conscript)\n  Delta   — Talent 3  (typical Sanctioned Primaris Psyker)\n  Gamma   — Talent 4\n  Beta    — Talent 4  (many Astropaths)\n  Alpha   — Talent 5  (Astartes Librarians, Black Ship candidates)\n  Alpha-Plus — Talent 6+ (beyond-play tier: Primarchs,\n    the most powerful Inquisitors, the worst Chaos sorcerers)\n\nLore: The Iota-to-Alpha-Plus assignment scale is drawn from the Adeptus Astra Telepathica's own grading of psychic potential as depicted across Dark Heresy-line material and repeated in Codex/Imperial Armour background; Black Library uses the same grades in passing (e.g. Eisenhorn-cycle and Ciaphas Cain references to Alpha-class talents). \"Alpha-Plus\" specifically denotes the rarest, most dangerous talents the Black Ships process. Grade names beyond Alpha/Beta/Gamma are RPG-line material only.\n\nDesign: Talent affecting four things (three skills plus the Perils resistance roll) sits at Basic's \"Medium Talent\" rate of 10 points/level (B90, three-to-five-skill bracket) rather than 5 (one-two skills) or 15 (six-nine skills) — the same rate Sorcery Talent itself uses (TSOR5, 10/level). Capped at level 6 for player characters; higher grades are GM-reference only, per the framework's \"Beyond play\" tier.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Talent",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"points_per_level": 10,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 2,
			"base_points": 0,
			"max_levels": 6
		},
		{
			"id": "tKIKfthrpRhPyb8kL",
			"name": "Warp Empowerment",
			"reference": "TSOR4",
			"local_notes": "A direct reskin of Sorcerous Empowerment (Thaumatology: Sorcery, TSOR4):\nthe psyker's raw, trained capacity to draw on and shape the warp, as\ndistinct from Psyker (Talent, TSOR5's Sorcery Talent analogue, below)\nwhich only sharpens related skills. Requires Unusual Background (Latent\nPsychic Potential). It is the prerequisite for every Warp Sorcery-tagged\nability in this file (see Witch-sight and each discipline below); the\nNavigator's Third Eye and the Pariah/Untouchable genes carry no Warp\nSorcery modifier and so need no Warp Empowerment.\n\nCap, cast per Thaumatology: Sorcery (TSOR): a psyker may only know a\npower whose full, modified point cost is no greater than the point cost\nof their own Warp Empowerment (10 + 10/level). A power bought at a\nhigher level (more dice, more range, etc.) costs proportionally more and\nmay outgrow a psyker's Empowerment even after they first learned it at a\ncheaper level — raising the level, and cost, of Warp Empowerment is how\na psyker \"grows into\" costlier or higher-level workings.\n\nImperial Assignment scale (Adeptus Astra Telepathica / Black Ships)\nmapped to Warp Empowerment level and the point cap that gives, worked\nout from this file's actual (Warp Sorcery -20% + Perils of the Warp)\nmodified costs. Attack powers are bought at lore levels (Smite and\nMolten Beam 12 levels at 162 points, Inferno 68, Crush 88, Fire Bolt 42, Flame Breath 33, Assail 28), so only a strong psyker can hold one\nat full strength; a weaker one buys the same power at fewer levels\n(cost is per level: Smite and Molten Beam 13.5, Inferno 8.5, Crush 11,\nFlame Breath 6.5, Fire Bolt and Assail 3.5).\n\n  Grade      | Empowerment level | Cap (10+10/lvl) | What the cap reaches\n  Iota       | 1                  | 20              | every non-attack power costing 20 or less: Endurance (2), Iron Arm (4), Warp Speed (4), Forewarning (11), Psychic Scream (12), Heroic Feats of Strength (12), Mind Probe (14), Foresight (18), Misfortune (18), Haemorrhage (19), Astrotelepathy (20). Attack powers only at reduced levels: Smite 1 level, Fire Bolt or Assail up to 5 levels, Crush 1 level\n  Zeta       | 1                  | 20              | as Iota; low Psyker Talent limits skill use, not what they can know\n  Eta        | 2                  | 30              | adds Mental Interrogation (21), Null Zone (23), Sanctuary (26), Terrify (28), Assail at full (28), Flame Breath at 4 levels (26). Smite 2 levels, Fire Bolt 8 levels, Crush 2 levels\n  Theta      | 3                  | 40              | adds Scrier's Gaze (35), Dominate (35), Gate of Infinity (40): the full non-attack roster, and Flame Breath at full (33). Fire Bolt 11 levels, Crush 3 levels, Smite 2 levels\n  Epsilon    | 5                  | 60              | Fire Bolt at full (42) with Flame Breath (33) and Assail (28) at full (a typical Wyrdvane battlefield psyker); Smite, Molten Beam or Crush at 4-5 levels\n  Delta      | 16                 | 170             | Smite and Molten Beam at full 12 levels (162), Inferno at full (68), Crush at full (88): the whole Imperial attack roster (a typical Sanctioned Primaris Psyker)\n  Gamma      | 16                 | 170             | as Delta, better skills from a higher Psyker Talent\n  Beta       | 18                 | 190             | as Delta with headroom to push one attack to 14 levels (189)\n  Alpha      | 20                 | 210             | full roster with headroom for high-level workings, up to 15 levels of Smite or Molten Beam (203) (Astartes Librarians, Black Ship candidates)\n  Alpha-Plus | 25+ (GM only)      | 260+            | 19 levels of Smite (257) and beyond; Vortex of Doom (230 as usually bought) needs level 22, so nothing below this grade reaches it\n\nA character need not buy Warp Empowerment up to their grade's full\nceiling: templates in this project buy only the level their actually-\nknown powers need, and note the grade's theoretical ceiling separately.\n\nLore: The Iota-to-Alpha-Plus assignment scale is drawn from the Adeptus Astra Telepathica's own grading of psychic potential as depicted across Dark Heresy-line material and repeated in Codex/Imperial Armour background; Black Library uses the same grades in passing (e.g. Eisenhorn-cycle and Ciaphas Cain references to Alpha-class talents). \"Alpha-Plus\" specifically denotes the rarest, most dangerous talents the Black Ships process. Only the Alpha/Beta/Gamma end of that ordering is well attested outside the RPG lines; the full Iota-to-Theta ordering used in the table above is Dark Heresy-line material and is not confirmed by the fiction.\n\nDesign: Sorcerous Empowerment (TSOR4) costs 10 base + 10/level (level 1 = 20 points, each further level +10) in the GCS master Thaumatology data; reused verbatim here under a 40k-appropriate name, since the underlying mechanic — \"how much warp-power you can channel at all, gating which spells/powers you may know by their modified cost\" — is exactly what an Imperial Assignment grade represents. The level-to-grade mapping above is derived, not asserted: it walks every power in this file's actual modified cost (recomputed under the new Warp Sorcery -20% modifier, see below) and finds the cheapest Empowerment level whose cap clears it, so \"Iota can only cast minor powers at full strength\" and \"Alpha can hold every attack power at full lore strength with room to spare\" are literally true of the numbers, not just flavour text. Levels were raised well past the old 1-7 range because attack powers now cost 162 points at lore scale rather than a handful.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Talent",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"points_per_level": 10,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 20,
			"base_points": 10
		},
		{
			"id": "tnppkCbpbuHIWf5rE",
			"name": "Witch-sight",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The first, mildest sign of the gift: an unlearned, passive prickling sense of daemonic taint, psychic activity, and warp corruption — how unsanctioned psykers are so often found, and how veteran Inquisitors and Space Marines notice a place has gone wrong before anything visible happens. Widely depicted; no single canonical source, consistent across Black Library and Dark Heresy-line material alike.\n\nDesign: Built as Detect (Psychic Activity & Daemonic Taint), an uncommon but not vanishingly rare category, base 10 per Basic's Detect cost table (B47-48). No Perils of the Warp: it is involuntary background sense, not a deliberate act of channelling. Full modified cost: 10 x 0.8 = 8 points; needs only Warp Empowerment level 1 (cap 20) — even an Iota-grade latent can have it, matching how often unsanctioned psykers are found by exactly this unlearned sense.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"modifiers": [
				{
					"id": "maMkzvSZ6MWL378BX",
					"name": "Warp Sorcery",
					"cost_adj": "-20%"
				}
			],
			"base_points": 10
		},
		{
			"id": "t2SHbB4pkhfo1_w9w",
			"name": "Social Stigma (Dangerous 'Ead)",
			"reference": "B155",
			"local_notes": "Second-Class Citizen-equivalent, within Ork society specifically.\n\nLore: Other Orks respect a Weirdboy's power and are visibly nervous around it in the same breath — nobody wants to be standing too close when the \"funny feeling\" starts, and a Weirdboy is watched, humoured, and occasionally \"helped\" by a Painboy's tools rather than fully trusted.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Social Stigma (Second-Class Citizen)-tier cost (B155), -5 points, reused here for the narrower \"everyone's a little afraid of you and it shows\" social cost, distinct from the Imperium's own Sanctioned Psyker Social Stigma since Ork society has no legal apparatus to register or license a Weirdboy in the first place.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Social",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"base_points": -5
		},
		{
			"id": "tGXcP4Nn7Uu6kv3P_",
			"name": "'Eadbanger",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A psychic bludgeon that a Weirdboy throws at a single target's skull, as far off as he can see a head (range penalties apply): the target's head bursts. On the tabletop the 'Eadbanger slays the model outright, and Ork fiction plays it exactly that grisly.\n\nDesign: Innate Attack (Crushing, 5/die, B61) bought at 10 levels, 10d(10) cr, with Malediction 2 (+150%, B106; the target resists with Will, it cannot dodge; normal Size and Speed/Range penalties apply), Armor Divisor (10) (+200%, B102), Warp Sorcery (-20%) and Perils (-10%). Net +320%. Per level: 5 x (1 + 1.5 + 2 - 0.20 - 0.10) = 21; full modified cost 210; needs Warp Empowerment level 20 (cap 210). Scale check: 10d(10) averages 35 before armour, so a Battle-Brother (torso DR 103, effective 10.3) takes about 25 penetrating crushing (x1) against HP 34, a Guardian (flak 30, effective 3) about 32 against HP 11, and a Carnifex (DR 150, effective 15, then Damage Reduction 3) about 7 against HP 70. Aimed at the skull the injury is x4 (B552), which is the head bursting. A lesser Weirdboy may buy fewer levels; the template buys all 10.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"modifiers": [
				{
					"id": "mQJkOimpjBvsuB38y",
					"name": "Malediction 2",
					"cost_adj": "+150%"
				},
				{
					"id": "m70zhBcd3L6HEmc-B",
					"name": "Armor Divisor (10)",
					"cost_adj": "+200%"
				},
				{
					"id": "mwp_p3L-PSGJKp6Sn",
					"name": "Warp Sorcery",
					"cost_adj": "-20%"
				},
				{
					"id": "mdBGG_WLt_QO3fP4T",
					"name": "Perils of the Warp",
					"cost_adj": "-10%"
				}
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 10
		},
		{
			"id": "taXZvJBxzg97Atgrg",
			"name": "Fists of Gork (an' Mork)",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Channels raw WAAAGH! force into a Weirdboy's own fists (or whoever's fists he's pointing at), letting even a bare-handed blow crack armour plate — the source of the tabletop \"Warpath\"/\"Fists of Gork\" tradition of a Weirdboy briefly turning his mob into something that hits far harder than it should.\n\nDesign: Striking ST (5/level, B) with Warp Sorcery/Perils, net -30% (the same derivation this project's Sanctic Daemonology uses for Hammerhand). Full modified cost: 5 x 0.7 = 3.5 points/level; needs Warp Empowerment level 1 (cap 20).",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"modifiers": [
				{
					"id": "mbvRWklX5377kh25A",
					"name": "Warp Sorcery",
					"cost_adj": "-20%"
				},
				{
					"id": "mEw9qN3mlt_GthMpK",
					"name": "Perils of the Warp",
					"cost_adj": "-10%"
				}
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 2
		},
		{
			"id": "TJMWSl8pCHG0pB3KP",
			"name": "Warpath",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Warpath is the two halves of the same WAAAGH!-fuelled blessing a Weirdboy throws over his own mob before a charge: sudden strength and sudden speed, together. Tabletop and Codex background both describe Weirdboyz using exactly this kind of squad-wide buff in the moments before Orks hit a line.\n\nDesign: Grouped as GCS sums the children: 12 (Heroic Feats of Strength) + 6 (Warp Speed, Mob, at level 1) = 18 points at its default starting level.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "t3D_MkLDAd1xBWW6f",
					"name": "Heroic Feats of Strength",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A howled WAAAGH! that fills a Weirdboy's whole mob with sudden, reckless strength for the charge.\n\nDesign: Basic's Heroic Feats of Strength (flat 10) with Area Effect (+50%) plus Warp Sorcery/Perils, net +20%. Full modified cost: 10 x 1.2 = 12 points; needs Warp Empowerment level 2 (cap 30) — the same build this project's Librarius: Might of Heroes uses.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mlGDxalDJIm4yxrOr",
							"name": "Area Effect",
							"cost_adj": "+50%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mNvRA4GeSsrOuoZOZ",
							"name": "Warp Sorcery",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						},
						{
							"id": "m3HqSdsGoYmd5Ve4s",
							"name": "Perils of the Warp",
							"cost_adj": "-10%"
						}
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "tGkvINAkbj9LVcJ1z",
					"name": "Warp Speed (Mob)",
					"local_notes": "Lore: The same burst quickens the whole mob's feet, turning a shambling charge into a genuine sprint.\n\nDesign: Increased Basic Speed (5 points per +0.25) with Area Effect (+50%) plus Warp Sorcery/Perils, net +20%. Full modified cost: 5 x 1.2 = 6 points/level; needs Warp Empowerment level 2 (cap 30).",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "m_vCYEsRME3pcTQh9",
							"name": "Area Effect",
							"cost_adj": "+50%"
						},
						{
							"id": "m7tAOn-b-zZZtt6_e",
							"name": "Warp Sorcery",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mI9AeZQlMKKxMf_EU",
							"name": "Perils of the Warp",
							"cost_adj": "-10%"
						}
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 1
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "tK91BhmqN308NCehg",
			"name": "Da Jump",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Weirdboy's signature and most feared trick: he grabs the nearest mob of Boyz, screams, and the whole lot vanish and reappear somewhere else on the battlefield an instant later — sometimes exactly where intended, sometimes scattered, occasionally (per the fiction's favourite running joke about Da Jump) several yards in the air. Weirdboyz and Da Jump-style warp effects recur in Ork fiction, played both as genuine tactical surprise and as slapstick; there is no single canonical mechanic.\n\nDesign: Built on Basic's own Warp advantage (B99, instant travel to any place you have seen/visualised, flat 100), matching this project's Gate of Infinity build, with Area Effect (+50%, B102, brings the whole mob) added on top, Preparation Required (-20%, a moment's howling concentration), Uncontrollable (Destructive, -30%, B116; the -10% Harmless version does not fit, since a mis-aimed mob is a real hazard: a Weirdboy can't always land the mob exactly where he meant to, the \"scatter\" of the tabletop, modelled honestly rather than smoothed over), Warp Sorcery (-20%) and the Severe Perils tier (-20%, a botched Jump is exactly the kind of interstellar-workings-adjacent disaster that tier exists for). Net: +50-20-30-20-20 = -40%. Full modified cost: 100 x 0.6 = 60 points — needing Warp Empowerment level 5 (cap 60). The Weirdboy template buys level 20 for the 'Eadbanger, so it can cast Da Jump anywhere. A less-developed Weirdboy at level 4 (cap 50) can cast it only inside a WAAAGH!, where WAAAGH! Gestalt raises his Empowerment to 5 (cap 60): the biggest trick in a Weirdboy's book then needs the mob behind him.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"modifiers": [
				{
					"id": "mAv1pQjeGcU1q65NZ",
					"name": "Area Effect",
					"cost_adj": "+50%"
				},
				{
					"id": "m_c_k1oEk4VFxXSFT",
					"name": "Preparation Required",
					"cost_adj": "-20%"
				},
				{
					"id": "mH-aVSuQ0mHVP4j8b",
					"name": "Uncontrollable (Destructive)",
					"reference": "B116",
					"cost_adj": "-30%"
				},
				{
					"id": "mYyJGd_mP1Tajyl7s",
					"name": "Warp Sorcery",
					"cost_adj": "-20%"
				},
				{
					"id": "m13K9Wr7w1vXl0VIr",
					"name": "Perils of the Warp (Severe)",
					"cost_adj": "-20%"
				}
			],
			"base_points": 100
		}
	],
